        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "614a0dc1-6b3b-513c-a4ab-5232193f271b",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- Organizations: Lamplighters Music Theatre\n- Works: Candide, The Merry Widow, Die Fledermaus, Of Thee I Sing, My Fair Lady, A Little Night Music\n- People: Orva Hoskinson, Ann Pool MacNab, Leonard Bernstein, Voltaire, Samuel Krachmalnick, George Maran, Paul Hindemith\n- Topics: light opera, Gilbert and Sullivan, Broadway, Harvard University\n\nFacts:\n- In 1759, Voltaire wrote the novella \"Candide,\" which later served as the basis for the operetta \"Candide.\"\n- In 1952, Orva Hoskinson and Ann Pool MacNab founded Lamplighters Music Theatre.\n- Lamplighters Music Theatre specializes in light opera, particularly the works of Gilbert and Sullivan.\n- Lamplighters Music Theatre has also performed works such as \"The Merry Widow,\" \"Die Fledermaus,\" \"Of Thee I Sing,\" \"My Fair Lady,\" \"Candide,\" and \"A Little Night Music.\"\n- During the 1950s, Samuel Krachmalnick first came to prominence as a conductor on Broadway.\n- During the 1950s, Samuel Krachmalnick received a Tony Award nomination for serving as music director of the original production of Leonard Bernstein's \"Candide.\"\n- Leonard Bernstein composed the music for the operetta \"Candide.\"\n- George Maran attended Harvard University, where his voice came to the attention of Leonard Bernstein and Paul Hindemith.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "28f4bd2c-07a1-51dc-83e1-d1dc188b9152",
        "text": "Page: Lamplighters Music Theatre. Sentence: Founded in 1952 by Orva Hoskinson and Ann Pool MacNab, the Lamplighters specialize in light opera, particularly the works of Gilbert and Sullivan, as well as such works as \"The Merry Widow\", \"Die Fledermaus\", \"Of Thee I Sing\", \"My Fair Lady\", \"Candide\", and \"A Little Night Music\".\nPage: Candide (operetta). Sentence: Candide is an operetta with music composed by Leonard Bernstein, based on the 1759 novella of the same name by Voltaire.\nPage: Samuel Krachmalnick. Sentence: He first came to prominence as a conductor on Broadway during the 1950s, notably earning a Tony Award nomination for his work as the music director of the original production of Leonard Bernstein's \"Candide\".\nPage: George Maran. Sentence: Maran attended Harvard University where he and his voice came to the attention of people such as Leonard Bernstein and Paul Hindemith.",
        "type": "DocumentChunk"
      },
